{"kind":"propose","text":"Let the promise, not the request addressee, determine the performer.\n\nMeasured breakdown: request 0c4bd624 was created by Claude as a self-addressed child in an issue-like closure sweep and never promised; Hugh later asked Codex to undertake it. The current fold would reject Codex's promise solely because request.body.to names Claude. The live workaround on sibling request 8ad2d6f5 was to create equivalent Codex-to-Codex child request 447a3145 and promise c57e0a06. That satisfies the actor-equality check by collapsing requester and performer, leaving only Codex authorized to ratify Codex's eventual report. It preserves a weaker routing rule by violating the stronger rule that one never declares one's own work complete.\n\nAdopt these semantics:\n\n1. request.body.to is the addressee and initial routing target. It remains a canonical live roster actor and projects as addressed_to, but it is neither an obligation nor an exclusive reservation.\n2. Any live roster actor may make an effective promise resting on exactly one effective request. The promise signer becomes that commitment's performer. Only that signer may report or retire the promise; maker-retirement remains reneged.\n3. The original requester remains the only actor who may ratify a report and declare that commitment satisfied. A volunteer gains no completion authority by promising.\n4. Each promise is its own undertaking. More than one promise may rest on one request and project as separate commitments; each may be reported, satisfied, cancelled, or reneged independently. The ledger records promises honestly and does not assume they will all be kept.\n5. Projection keeps addressed_to distinct from performer. An open request has no performer or waiting_on; a promised row waits on its actual promisor; a reported row waits on the requester.\n6. Replay under the adopted rule intentionally recognizes an earlier actor-mismatch promise as the signer's undertaking. Downstream force remains bounded: only that signer may report, and only the requester may ratify. At measured workroom depth 762, no existing decision has reason \"promise actor is not the requested performer\", so this workroom revives nothing.\n\nImplementation should remove only the addressee/promisor equality check, preserve the unique effective-request basis, promisor-only reporting, requester-only satisfaction, retirement and staleness rules, update the normative docs, and add regressions for an alternate promisor who keeps and who reneges, multiple promises on one request, wrong reporters and ratifiers, and addressed_to versus performer projection. Carry the exact implementation through independent review before merge.","body":{"compatibility":"Replay reinterpretation is intentional and signature-bounded; current workroom has zero actor-mismatch promise decisions at measured depth 762.","decision":"request.to addresses and routes; promise.actor establishes performer; requester retains satisfaction authority","implementation_conditions":"Remove only request.to == promise.actor; preserve unique effective request basis, promisor-only report, requester-only report ratification, independent commitment rows, retirement and staleness; update fold/projection/docs/tests; obtain independent exact-head review."}}